CLI Arguments
===============================================================================

The CLI argument context exposes raw Unicode argument text as read-only resources.

```mech
+> @args := cli/args
first := @args/1
```

1. Paths and values
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Paths are one-based positive canonical decimal indices: 1, 2, 3, and so on.
Index 1 selects the first argument after the executable. Zero, leading zeroes,
signs, spaces, fractions, extra path segments and overflowing indices are rejected.
Missing arguments and arguments that are not valid Unicode produce errors.
An empty argument is valid and returns an empty string. No numeric conversion or
lossy Unicode conversion is performed.

The standard backend reads process argv, excluding the executable at index zero.
It does not remove launcher flags or source filenames. A launcher that parses its
own options must inject only its user-argument snapshot through CliBackend::argument.
The Mech interpreter CLI and generated hosted launchers do not yet provide this
forwarding boundary; their options are not an application argument interface.

2. Planning and capabilities
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Planning validates the index and supplies a string placeholder without reading
argv. The runtime captures the actual read through the same observation and
capability system as cli/env. Args do not require an input driver. Writes and
sends are rejected.

Importing the context grants no authority, and the legacy default CLI profiles
do not grant args reads. Grant specific indices in configuration:

```mech
config := {
  run: {
    grants: [{ target: "cli/args" operations: ["read"] paths: ["1"] }]
  }
}
```

The explicit :cli/args capability profile grants read access to all argument
indices. Configured CLI aliases use their instance name, such as term/args.

3. Embedding
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Custom CLI backends implement argument(index) using a user-argument snapshot;
the snapshot excludes the executable and launcher policy flags. Existing backends
can omit this optional method, in which case reads report arguments unavailable.
No argument contents need to be read during compilation or planning.
